# The annotations of the game image: the single source of truth for src/game/*.asm (kit/mkgame.py).
# The format is in kit/mkgame.py, class Annotations. One line an entry:
#
#   @module NAME ; title            the entries from here on go to NAME.asm, the title heads the file
#   @about a paragraph              the module file's header text, one paragraph a line
#   $ADDR c Name ; title            a routine
#   > a description line
#   $ADDR b Name ; title            data (w words, t text, s space; "b Name 8" rows of 8)
#   @res graphics/NAME.bin          the entry is a resource: src/res/..., INCBIN
#   $ADDR Name ; comment            a label and comment inside an entry: on an instruction, or on the data line
#                                   that starts at ADDR (a data entry's rows are cut there)
#   @isub $ADDR LD HL,-6            the instruction written so
#   @equ $ADDR Name [N] ; comment    a variable past the image: an EQU, its operands by name
#   @inline $ADDR                   a routine that reads its data after the CALL

@equ $4000 Screen 6144 ; the display file
@equ $5800 Attributes 768 ; the screen attributes
@equ $E000 CharMap 640 ; the play area's character map: 20 rows of 32 character codes
@equ $E200 MenuAttrs 768 at $9ED8,$9EFA,$8886,$8C04 ; the options screen's attributes, built over CharMap's end and DrawMarks
@equ $E300 DrawMarks 768 ; which characters to redraw, laid out like the attributes (2: two passes)
@equ $E500 MenuPixels 6144 at $9EED,$9EF0,$9EFD,$8889,$8B9B ; the options screen's pixels, built over the play buffers
@equ $E600 ColourBuffer 640 ; the play area's attributes, 20 rows of 32
@equ $E900 PixelBuffer 5120 ; the play area's pixels, 160 rows of 32 bytes
@equ $FD00 LevelGrid 253 ; the level's cells: 17 bytes a row (15 cells and two guards), the grid proper from $FD23
@equ $FDFD Im2Jump 3 ; JP InterruptHandler, where the IM 2 table points
@equ $FE00 Im2Table 257 ; the IM 2 vector table, 257 bytes of $FD

@module menu ; The title screen, the options panel, the demo, the practice levels
$6000 b TitlePicture 32 ; The title screen: a 6912-byte Spectrum screen copied whole to $4000
> TitleScreen `$A729` copies these 6912 bytes to the screen (`LD HL,$6000`, `LD DE,$4000`, `LD BC,$1B00`, `LDIR` at `$A749`) before the title tune: the DEFLEKTOR logo, a laser beam bouncing between crystal blocks, and the credit line "WRITTEN BY COSTA PANAYI (c)1987 VORTEX SOFTWARE" on row 23. 6144 pixel bytes in the screen's own order, then 768 attributes (8 values: `$02`, `$03`, `$05`, `$06`, `$07`, `$1D`, `$3A`, `$42`; no FLASH).
> Read in every recording that showed the title (`c1g`, `controls`, `gameover`, `kempston`, `menu`, `mine`, `overload`), written by nothing: the image's bytes are the picture. The editable form is screens/title.png.
$6000 ; pixels, the top third (rows 0-7)
$6800 ; pixels, the middle third (rows 8-15)
$7000 ; pixels, the bottom third (rows 16-23)
$7800 ; the attributes, 24 rows of 32
@res screens/title.bin
$7B00 b Gap7B00 32 ; 128 bytes of 0 between the title picture and the panel picture: no code reads or writes them
> No instruction addresses `$7B00-$7B7F` and no recording reads or writes a byte of it. It pads the panel picture to start at `$7B80`, so that it ends at `$7FFF` (1152 bytes): a leftover of the layout.
@res leftovers/gap-7b00.bin

@module panel ; The panel, the score, the COMPUTER messages
$7B80 b PanelPicture 32 ; The play panel of rows 20-23: 1024 pixel bytes in plain rows, then 128 attributes
> DrawPanel `$A50C` (from LevelPanel `$8432`, at each new game and level start) copies 32 bytes a pixel row to the screen, the 8 rows of character row 20, then rows 21, 22 and 23 (the screen address advanced by `INC D` and `E + 32`), then the 128 attributes to `$5A80-$5AFF`. So the bytes are a linear picture of 32 pixel rows of 32 bytes, top to bottom, followed by 4 rows of 32 attributes (`$02`-`$07`, no BRIGHT, no FLASH).
> The panel: "LEVEL ." with the two digit boxes (columns 6-7, filled by SetUpLevel `$843C` from LevelDigits `$88B0`); LIVES with three magenta bars (row 21, columns 10-15, hollowed by LoseLife `$866A`); OVERLOAD and ENERGY with their dotted bars (rows 21 and 23, columns 17-24, filled by `$A683` / `$A613`); SCORE and HIGH with their boxes (columns 26-31); COMPUTER with the message box (row 23, columns 1-14, the scroll of InterruptHandler `$A32A`).
> Read 67 times (`c1g`, `controls`, `energy`, `kempston`, `menu` and 59 RZX segments); never written. The editable form is screens/panel.png (256 x 32, the attributes in the palette).
$7B80 ; character row 20: pixel rows 0-7
$7C80 ; character row 21
$7D80 ; character row 22
$7E80 ; character row 23
$7F80 ; the attributes of rows 20-23 (to $5A80)
@res screens/panel.bin

@module beam ; The beam: the laser, the receiver, the trace
$8000 b BeamGlyphBytes 4 ; The beam's glyphs round a vertex: 4 bytes for each direction, two sets of 16 directions
> BeamVertex `$906E` (at `$90C7`) and the outgoing pass (`$91E3`) point DE at `$8000` + 4 x direction + BeamGlyphSet `$8381` (`LD D,$80`): set 0 at `$8000`, set 1 (`$8381` = `$40`, the flashing beam while the energy is critical) at `$8040`. The four bytes are the characters up-left, up-right, down-left and down-right of the vertex.
> A byte's bits 7-6 say which pass draws it: 00 the incoming pass BeamInChar `$9278`, 01 the outgoing pass BeamOutChar `$94FD`, 10 the corner pass BeamCornerChar `$9440` (a slanted beam cutting a character's corner); bits 5-0 the glyph, BeamGlyphs `$D500` + 8 x glyph. The glyphs used are 0-38. Read in every recording with a beam; the editable form is tables/beam-glyph-bytes.csv.
$8000 ; set 0, directions 0-3 (east to south-south-east)
$8010 ; set 0, directions 4-7
$8020 ; set 0, directions 8-11
$8030 ; set 0, directions 12-15
$8040 ; set 1 (the flashing beam), directions 0-3
$8050 ; set 1, directions 4-7
$8060 ; set 1, directions 8-11
$8070 ; set 1, directions 12-15
@res tables/beam-glyph-bytes.bin
$8080 b OutBounceTable 8 ; A slanted beam's bounce in the outgoing pass: 8 bytes for each new direction
> BeamReflectOut `$960D` (at `$965C`, a slope or diagonal meeting a wall or a yellow polariser) takes `$8080` + 8 x the new direction: two words, the offsets from the wall character met (CharMapPointer `$8914`) in the character map of the two characters to test, then four directions by which of them are walls (`$31-$72`): none, the first, the second, both. BeamCornerChar (at `$94E9`, two corners met) reads only the last byte, `$8087` + 8 x direction.
> The rows of the axis directions 0, 4, 8 and 12 are never read (an axis beam grazes or goes straight back: `$8190`); `$80CF` and `$80DF` (the "both" of directions 9 and 11) were read by no recording. Editable form: tables/out-bounce.csv.
@res tables/out-bounce.bin
$8100 b InCornerTable 16 ; A diagonal beam's bounce in the incoming pass: 16 bytes for each of the diagonals 2, 6, 10, 14
> ReflectInCorner `$93F2` (`$93F4`): InBounceTable gives `$80` + n for a diagonal, and `$8100` + n holds two words (the offsets, from the wall character met (CharMapPointer `$8914`), of the characters above or below and beside it) and four answers of three bytes (the new direction, dx, dy) by which of them are walls: none (+4), the first (+7), the second (+10), both (+13). None or both send the beam back (OVERLOAD), one turns it a quarter.
> No play reads it: the trace's first vertex is the only one whose incoming pass meets a wall the outgoing pass before it has not (work/docs/integrate/corner_level1.py), and that vertex is the laser's front, on an axis. Read only on a clone, `corner` (game0.szx with the beam's start `$8376-$8378` POKEd to x 10, y 16, direction 10: 342 passes, the beam turned a quarter to direction 14). Editable form: tables/in-corner.csv.
$8100 ; direction 2 (south-east)
$8110 ; direction 6 (south-west)
$8120 ; direction 10 (north-west)
$8130 ; direction 14 (north-east)
@res tables/in-corner.bin
$8140 b InBounceTable 3 ; A slanted beam's bounce in the incoming pass: the new direction and the step, 3 bytes a direction
> BeamReflectIn `$938F` (at `$93DE`, ReflectInSlant): `$8140` + 3 x direction. Byte 0 is the bounced direction (a slope's mirror image off the wall it can meet: 1 to 7, 7 to 1, 9 to 15, 15 to 9 off an upright wall; 3 to 13, 5 to 11, 11 to 5, 13 to 3 off a level one), or `$80` + n for a diagonal (InCornerTable `$8100` + n decides); bytes 1-2 the step (dx, dy) to the vertex the beam leaves from, given to BeamMove `$9235`.
> Only the slopes' and diagonals' rows are read (the axis rows 0, 4, 8, 12 are there for the index, and the recordings read the slope rows only: 21 949 bounces in 33 RZX segments). Editable form: tables/in-bounce.csv.
@res tables/in-bounce.bin
$8170 b BeamDeltas 2 ; The step of each of the 16 beam directions: dx, dy in half-cells (signed), 0 east, clockwise
> (2,0) (2,1) (1,1) (1,2) (0,2) (-1,2) (-1,1) (-2,1) (-2,0) (-2,-1) (-1,-1) (-1,-2) (0,-2) (1,-2) (1,-1) (2,-1): a step is one cell along the axes, a character on the diagonals, two by one on the slopes. Read by BeamStep `$9226` (`$9230`, 2 x direction), FindLaser `$8AC1` (`$8AF7`, the half-cell in front of the laser: 8 x the laser's direction index) and OpenReceiver `$8B3F` (`$8B6A`, the cell in front of the receiver).
> Editable form: tables/beam-deltas.csv.
@res tables/beam-deltas.bin
$8190 w GrazeOffsets 4 ; The character across an axis beam's line: 8 offsets in the character map
> When an axis beam meets a wall character, the character across the beam line decides between grazing and stopping: BeamAbsorbIn `$9344` (at `$9357`), BeamReflectIn (`$93A2`) and the outgoing tests (`$95D5`, `$9620`) read the word at `$8190` + 2 x (the character's number in the pass - 1) + direction, so word (k - 1) + direction / 2: +31, -33, -31, -33, +33, -31, +33, +31 (the character map is 32 wide).
> For direction 12 the third and fourth characters of a pass would read `$81A0`-`$81A3`, the start of PolariserCells; whether the passes ever reach them there is not known from the recordings. Editable form: tables/graze-offsets.csv.
@res tables/graze-offsets.bin
$81A0 w PolariserCells 3 ; The three other characters of a polariser: 3 offsets for each of its four characters
> PolariserPassIn (`$9320`) and the outgoing pass (`$959E`): a beam along a polariser's axis lights the polariser's other three characters. The row is `$81A0` + 6 x (character code AND 3); the polariser characters `$31-$70` run top left, top right, bottom left, bottom right from `$x1`, so row 0 is the bottom-right character (-33, -32, -1), row 1 the top-left (+1, +32, +33), row 2 the top-right (+31, +32, -1), row 3 the bottom-left (+1, -32, -31).
> Editable form: tables/polariser-cells.csv.
@res tables/polariser-cells.bin

@module mainloop ; The main loop: the level's set-up and the pass
$81B8 b ExplosionSlots 4 ; Seven explosion slots of 4 bytes: the cell's grid address (high, low), y, x; high byte 0 = free
> A burning cell (BeamBurn at `$8F43`: the six first slots looked at, the seventh taken when all are full) or the receiver's door (OpenReceiver at `$8B7D`, the first slot written backwards from `$81BB`). PassEnd `$85AE` (at `$85F1`) steps each slot's cell through the codes `$57`, `$58-$5C` (one a pass) and empties it at `$5D` (CellsLeft - 1, the slot freed). SetUpLevel clears the 28 bytes (`$84E1`).
> Zero in the image (a work area); read and written in every recording with a level; `$81D1-$81D3` (the seventh slot's low, y, x) were written but never read back.
$81B8 ; slot 1 (also the receiver's door)
$81BC ; slots 2-7

@module sound ; Sound: the beeper effects, the title tune
$81D4 b BurnSound 5 ; PlaySound record: a sweep when a cell starts to burn (also SoundFlags bit 0)
> Kind 1 (PlaySweep `$A85E`): H 2, L 4, D `$2E`, E 9. PassEnd plays it directly (`$8657`) on a pass with a new explosion (BurnSoundFlag `$837B`); PlaySoundFlags `$A8A7` would also play it for SoundFlags bit 0, which nothing sets.
> The eight records `$81D4-$81FB` are one resource, tables/sounds.csv (5 bytes each: the kind, then four parameters).
@res tables/sounds.bin
$81D9 b MineSound 5 ; PlaySound record: SoundFlags bit 1, the beam on a mine (a falling sweep)
> Kind 1: H `$FE` (-2: the delay grows, the pitch falls), L 4, D `$46`, E 2. Played by the interrupt (PlaySoundFlags) every frame while BeamOnMine sets bit 1 (`$8F75`).
@res tables/sounds.bin
$81DE b LaserBackSound 5 ; PlaySound record: SoundFlags bit 2, the beam back in the laser
> Kind 1: H `$0A`, L 3, D `$14`, E 1. Played by the interrupt while UpdateOverload `$A683` keeps bit 2 set (`$A6A9`).
@res tables/sounds.bin
$81E3 b BurningNoise 5 ; PlaySound record: SoundFlags bit 3, a cell burning (noise)
> Kind 2 (PlayNoise `$A87A`): D 2 (the period mask), E 4 (bursts), C `$0A` (the length mask), and the opcode `$A2` (`AND D`) that PlayNoise stores at NoiseOp `$A89F`. Bit 3 is set by PassEnd while an explosion is at `$58-$59` (`$860F`).
@res tables/sounds.bin
$81E8 b ChargeWarble 5 ; PlaySound record: SoundFlags bit 4, the laser charging (a warble)
> Kind 0 (PlayTone `$A83F`): L `$8C`, H 2 (TwoTone `$A843`: 2 x 256 loops). The last two bytes (`$81EB-$81EC`, zeros) are not read: a tone takes two parameters. Bit 4 is set by ChargeLaser (`$A5DA`).
@res tables/sounds.bin
$81ED b GremlinSound 5 ; PlaySound record: a gremlin killed, and each life counted in the level-complete bonus
> Kind 1: H 2, L 4, D `$50`, E `$1E`. PassEnd (`$85B1`, GremlinSoundFlag `$837C` set by CursorFire) and LevelCompletePause (`$884D`, one per life left).
@res tables/sounds.bin
$81F2 b ChargedSound 5 ; PlaySound record: the laser charged, and twice at the ending
> Kind 1: H 8, L 4, D 3, E `$64`. ChargeLaser (`$A60C`) when the bar is full, TheEnd (`$8896`, `$889C`), EndingScreen (`$A949`).
@res tables/sounds.bin
$81F7 b ContactSound 5 ; PlaySound record: CONTACT!, the beam in the receiver
> Kind 1: H 2, L 4, D 5, E `$DC`. LevelComplete `$87D4` (at `$87E3`).
@res tables/sounds.bin
$81FC b Spare81FC ; One byte of 0 after the sound records: never read or written
> No instruction addresses it and no recording reads or writes it.

@module controls ; The controls: keys, joysticks, pause, quit
$81FD b ControlMethod ; The control method: 0 keyboard, 1 Sinclair, 2 Kempston, 3 Protek / AGF (1 in the image)
> Written by OptionsMenu (`$A823`, keys 1-4); read by ReadControls `$9869` (`$9869`), CursorFire (`$9A5F`) and OptionsMenu (`$A775`, the highlight). The image holds 1 (Sinclair): the value of the last session the file was saved from, kept until the player presses 1-4.
$81FE b Spare81FE ; One byte of 0 between ControlMethod and Level: never read or written
> No instruction addresses it and no recording reads or writes it.

@module mainloop
$81FF b Level ; The level slot being played: 0-59 the levels, 60-62 practice P1-P3 (60 after the last level: the ending)
> Set by NewGame (`$8426`, 0 or `$3C`), counted up by LevelComplete (`$87E9`, `$87ED`) and by the demo (`$8562`, `$8566`), compared by LevelCompletePause (`$8875`: 60 the ending, 63 the end of practice); read by SetUpLevel (`$843C`, `$84C6`: the digits and LevelSettings), BuildLevel `$891A` (LevelData + `$87` x Level) and ResetBars (`$A567`); changed by the dormant cheat LevelCheat `$9832`.

@module render ; The renderer: the grid into characters, the buffers, the copies, the wipes, the printers
$8200 b CellTable 4 ; The four characters of each cell code $00-$5C: top left, top right, bottom left, bottom right
> RenderGrid `$8960` (`$8975` and, for a turning element, `$89D4`) takes `$8200` + 4 x code (bit 7 cleared) and puts the four character codes into the character map `$E000`; DrawMarkedChars `$8A09` draws each character from CellGraphics `$D700` + 8 x character in the colour CellColours `$DF00` + character.
> Mirrors `$01-$10` use characters `$01-$30` (16 drawings sharing some quarters); polarisers `$11-$18` (cyan) and `$19-$20` (yellow) characters `$31-$70`, four of their own each; the walls `$21-$2F` (absorbing) and `$30-$3E` (reflecting) are built of one character, `$71` or `$72`, in the quarters the code names, `$00` elsewhere; cell `$40` `$74-$77`, mine `$41` `$78-$7B`, prism `$42` `$7C-$7F`; `$43-$46` `$80-$8F` (the four lasers drawn in cyan: no level holds these codes); lasers `$47-$4A` `$90-$9F`; receivers `$4B-$4E` `$A0-$AF`; the fibre-optic ends `$50-$57` `$B0-$BF` (a pair shares its four characters); the explosion frames `$58-$5C` `$C0-$D3`.
> Never read in the recordings: `$29`, `$2A` (the diagonal wall pairs), `$3F` (the cell's characters), `$43-$46`, `$4F` (four blanks): no level holds them. Editable form: tables/cell-table.csv.
$8200 ; $00 empty, $01-$03 mirrors
$8240 ; $10 mirror, $11-$18 cyan polarisers, $19-$1B yellow polarisers
$8270 ; $1C-$20 yellow polarisers, $21-$2F absorbing walls
$82C0 ; $30-$3E reflecting walls, $3F unused
$8300 ; $40 cell, $41 mine, $42 prism, $43-$46 unused (cyan lasers)
$8318 ; $47-$4A lasers, $4B-$4E receivers, $4F unused
$8340 ; $50-$57 fibre-optic ends (pairs $50/$51 ... $56/$57)
$8360 ; $58-$5C the explosion's five frames
@res tables/cell-table.bin

@module mainloop
$8374 b HighlightX ; The x (half-cells) of the cell HighlightCell colours: the laser's centre, or the receiver's
> Written by FindLaser (`$8AE2`, the laser's centre) and by the beam at the receiver (`$8F9E`, before LevelDone); read by HighlightCell `$8FCA` (`$8FD6`). 0 in the image, as are all the variables to `$8381`.
$8375 b HighlightY ; The y (half-cells) of the highlighted cell
> Written at `$8AE8` and `$8FA4`, read by HighlightCell (`$8FCA`).
$8376 b BeamStartX ; The beam's start x: the half-cell in front of the laser
> Written by FindLaser (`$8B02`), read by MainLoop (`$857B`) to start TraceBeam.
$8377 b BeamStartY ; The beam's start y
> Written by FindLaser (`$8B12`), read by MainLoop (`$8581`).
$8378 b BeamStartDirection ; The laser's direction 0, 4, 8 or 12; $FF: no laser, or the energy drained (no beam)
> Written by FindLaser (`$8ADB` `$FF`, `$8AF3` the direction) and EnergyDrained (`$A67F`, `$FF`); read by MainLoop (`$8571`).
$8379 b DemoFlag ; $FF while the demo runs (the options panel's 5), else 0
> Cleared by TitleLoop (`$840D`), set by OptionsMenu (`$A7D0`); read by the main loop (`$8559`: the next level once charged), PauseGame (`$973A`), ReadKeys (`$9819`: DEMO ABORTED) and ResetBars (`$A561`: the DEMO message).
$837A b MirrorCount ; The number of mirrors $01-$10 in the level (turning ones not counted)
> Counted by SetUpLevel (`$8537`); read by ChargeLaser (`$A598`): with 30 or more the charge scrambles each mirror with a chance of 1 in 8, else 1 in 2.
$837B b BurnSoundFlag ; Set when a cell starts to explode this pass: PassEnd plays BurnSound
> Set at `$8603`, read at `$8650` (BurnSound), cleared at `$85BC`.
$837C b GremlinSoundFlag ; Set by CursorFire when a gremlin is killed: PassEnd plays GremlinSound
> Cleared by SetUpLevel (`$84B0`) and PassEnd (`$85B9`), read by PassEnd (`$85AE`), set by CursorFire (`$9ADE`).
$837D b Lives ; The lives left (3 at a new game and after each level)
> Set to 3 by NewGame (`$841B`) and LevelCompletePause (`$8872`), counted down by LoseLife (`$86A6`-`$86AA`, `$86CD`), read by LevelCompletePause (`$882A`, the bonus).
$837E b EnergyCritical ; $FF once the energy bar is down to its last byte: the beam flashes
> Cleared by SetUpLevel (`$84AA`), set by DrainEnergy (`$A623`); read by MainLoop (`$8587`), which then sets BeamGlyphSet from bit 3 of FrameCounter.
$837F b FrameCounter ; Counted up by the interrupt every frame (the game path)
> InterruptHandler `$A32A` (`$A33C`, `$A340`); read by MainLoop (`$858D`) for the beam's flashing.
$8380 b CellsLeft ; The cells ($40) left to burn; $FF after the receiver's door is opened
> Counted by SetUpLevel (`$853B`), counted down by PassEnd (`$861D`) when an explosion ends; 0 makes PassEnd call OpenReceiver (`$85DB`), which sets it to `$FF` (`$8B97`).
$8381 b BeamGlyphSet ; 0 or $40: the offset of the glyph set in BeamGlyphBytes ($40 the flashing set)
> Written by MainLoop (`$8598`: `$40` on the frames with bit 3 of FrameCounter set while EnergyCritical, else 0); read by BeamVertex (`$90C7`) and the outgoing pass (`$91E3`) as the low byte added to `$8000` + 4 x direction.

# Deflektor, annotator 01-main: $8382-$8FFF (the entry, the title loop and new game, the level set-up, the main loop,
# lives, level complete, game over, the level builder, the renderer, the wipes, the print routines, the start of the
# beam trace). The @equ lines this fragment needs are listed in work/ann/01-main.md (the lead writes them).

@isub $870B LD HL,$C814

@module entry ; The entry, the title loop, a new game
@about The game is entered at GameEntry $8382 (the mover at $FD00 jumps there with SP = $FFFF): Start sets up IM 2 ($A2FA) and falls into TitleLoop, the loop every game ends in: the hall-of-fame check of the last score ($9BC0), the title and the options panel ($A729), then NewGame (three lives, level 1, or the practice level P1 when the options panel's 6 set PracticeFlag) and LevelPanel, which draws the bottom panel afresh and falls into the level set-up $843C.
@about LevelSettings $8385 is the per-level table the set-up reads: the energy bar's rate and the number of gremlins, two bytes for each of the 64 level slots.
$8382 c GameEntry ; The game's entry: JP Start
> The mover of the last loader stage jumps here after moving the game to `$6000-$DFFF` (SP = `$FFFF`). One `JP` over the per-level table LevelSettings to Start `$8408`.
> Run once a load (`c1` and its game window `c1g`; the first instruction of `c1g`).
> Witness: L1 2 (c1, c1g).
$8385 b LevelSettings 16 ; The energy rate and the gremlin count of each level: 2 bytes for each of the 64 level slots
> Read by SetUpLevel `$843C` (at `$84CD`): slot `Level` x 2. Byte 0 goes to `$A63C`, the operand of the `CP $64` at `$A63B` in the energy routine `$A613`: the energy bar loses one pixel each time the interrupt's tick `$A506` reaches it, so it is the number of frames per pixel (70-140 in levels 1-60, 255 in the practice slots). Byte 1 goes to `$AB95`, the number of gremlin records `$AB63` the gremlin code walks (`$9AB6`, `$ABAD`): 0-5 in levels 1-59, 8 in level 60, 0 / 8 / 0 / 0 in the four practice slots.
> Levels 1-60 are slots 0-59; slots 60-62 are the practice levels P1-P3 (PracticeFlag); slot 63 is never set up (LevelCompletePause `$887D` returns to the title when the level number reaches 63). Each row of the listing is eight levels.
@res tables/level-settings.bin
$8385 ; levels 1-8
$8395 ; levels 9-16
$83A5 ; levels 17-24
$83B5 ; levels 25-32
$83C5 ; levels 33-40
$83D5 ; levels 41-48
$83E5 ; levels 49-56
$83F5 ; levels 57-60, practice P1-P3, the unused slot 63
$8405 ; unused: no code or recording reads them
$8407 b PracticeFlag ; $FF in practice mode (the options panel's 6), else 0
> Cleared by TitleLoop `$840C`, set by the options panel (`$A7E5`, key 6). Read by NewGame (`$841E`: the start slot 60, P1), SetUpLevel (`$84DA`: the score cleared each level), LoseLife (`$86A0`: no life is taken), DrawMarkedChars (`$8A68`: the PRACTICE MODE line), `$9BCA`, `$A6E9` and `$A7E5`.
> The `menu` recording's key 6 did not take (its screen 7750 shows LEVEL 01). The practice path is played by `practice` (`work/docs/dfA/rec/practice`, 6 PRACTICE from `menu.szx`): NewGame `$8424` and the practice set-up `$84DA` once, level P1 (slot 60).
$8408 c Start ; Interrupts off, IM 2 on (the jump at $FDFD and the vector table), then into TitleLoop
> `DI`, then `$A2FA` (the IM 2 set-up: `JP $A32A` at `$FDFD`, the table `$FE00-$FF00`, I = `$FE`). Falls into TitleLoop; interrupts stay off until LevelPanel's `EI` (`$843B`).
> Run once a load (`c1`, `c1g`).
> Witness: L1 2 (c1, c1g).
$840C c TitleLoop ; Demo and practice off, the hall-of-fame check, the title and options panel, then a new game
> The place every game ends: GameOver (`$8711`), the demo's last level (`$856E`), the end of practice (`$887F`), the ending (`$88AD`) and the abort keys (`$982C`) jump here.
> Clears the demo flag `$8379` and PracticeFlag; `$9BC0` (the score `$9B3E` against the hall of fame, the name entry); `$A729` (the title, the options panel; it returns when a game is to start, with `$8379` set by key 5 DEMO or PracticeFlag by key 6). Falls into NewGame.
> Witness: L1 15 (c1, c1g, controls, energy-out, gameover, hof, kempston, menu, mine, nameedit, overload, rzx344752).
$8413 ; the hall-of-fame check of the last game's score
$8416 ; the title and the options panel
$8419 c NewGame ; Three lives, the first level (or practice slot 60), the saved score cleared, then LevelPanel
> Lives `$837D` = 3; Level `$81FF` = 0, or `$3C` (P1) when PracticeFlag is set; two Delays (about 24 frames); `$9B30` clears the score saved at the level's start (`$9B44`, five spaces), so SetUpLevel starts the game's score at 0. Falls into LevelPanel.
> An entry of its own: the `energy` and `kempston` recordings start from a snapshot taken inside `$A729`, so their L1s mark a routine root here.
> Witness: L1 11 (c1, c1g, controls, energy, energy-out, kempston, menu, practice).
$8426 NewGameLevel ; Level = 0, or $3C for practice
$8429 ; two Delays
$842C NewGameDelay2 ; the second Delay
$842F ; the saved score cleared: the game starts at 0
$8432 c LevelPanel ; Draw the bottom panel afresh, blank the COMPUTER line, interrupts on; into SetUpLevel
> From NewGame and LevelCompletePause (`$8882`, after a level's bonus). `$A50C` copies the panel's picture `$7B80` (four character rows, the LEVEL, LIVES, OVERLOAD, ENERGY, SCORE, HIGH and COMPUTER fields, three full life bars) to the screen's rows 20-23 with its attributes; MessageText `$A10E` = `$A199` (a blank line of three spaces that loops); `EI`. Falls into SetUpLevel.
> Witness: L1 92 (c1, c1g, controls, energy, energy-out, hof, kempston, menu, nameedit, practice, L x20, rzx x59).

@module mainloop ; The level set-up, the main loop's pass, level complete
@about SetUpLevel $843C prints the level number, clears the pass's flags, the gremlin records and the explosion slots, reads the level's settings, puts back the score saved at the level's start, builds the level ($891A) and wipes it onto the screen ($8CA2), resets the bars ($A52F: CHARGING LASER), counts the cells and mirrors, finds the laser and the cursor's first place ($8AC1), then runs MainLoop.
@about MainLoop $854A is one pass: the keys ($97F0), $999F, the grid drawn into the buffers ($8960); while the laser charges no beam is traced (in the demo every pass shows the next level instead); else the beam is traced from the laser (TraceBeam $8ED6); $9A57, $99C6, the gremlins ($AB9F), the play area copied to the screen ($8C10); then PassEnd: the overload ($A683) and a life lost (LoseLife), the energy ($A613), the receiver reached (LevelComplete), the receiver's door blown when the last cell burns (OpenReceiver), the burning cells' explosions. The pass never waits for the frame: 4.85 frames a pass in the RZX run.
@about LevelComplete $87D4: CONTACT!, the screen turned magenta, the remaining energy counted into the score (10 points a bar pixel), 500 points a life left, the score saved, the lives back to three, then the next level, the ending after level 60, or the title after the third practice level.
$843C c SetUpLevel ; Set up the level in Level $81FF and play it: digits, flags, settings, score, build, wipe, counts, laser
> From LevelPanel (falls in), the demo's next level (`$856B`) and LoseLife (`$86D1`, the level again after a life lost).
> 1. The LEVEL digits: A = Level + 1 split into tens (B) and units; for the practice slots (Level >= 60) B = 10, the glyph P, and the units Level - 59 (P1-P3). Each digit is eight lines of LevelDigits `$88B0` + 8 x digit, drawn on lines 4-7 of character row 20 and lines 0-3 of row 21 (`$5486` / `$50A6` for the tens, the next column for the units): LEVEL 01 in the panel.
> 2. Clears the cursor's cell `$96C3` / `$96C4`, LevelDone, LifeLost, BeamOnMine, BeamInLaser, the energy-critical flag `$837E`, SoundFlags `$A8A6` and the gremlin-sound flag `$837C`.
> 3. The eight gremlin records at `$AB63` (6 bytes each): byte 0 = `$41`, `$4B`, `$55` ... (`$41` + 10 k), byte 1 = 0.
> 4. LevelSettings: byte 0 to `$A63C` (the energy rate operand), byte 1 to `$AB95` (the gremlin count). In practice the saved score is cleared (`$9B30`).
> 5. The seven explosion slots `$81B8-$81D3` cleared; the score `$9B3E` (five digits) = the score saved at the level's start `$9B44`, so a level begun again after a life lost starts from the score it began with; the score's field on the screen (lines 3-7 of row 21, columns 26-31) cleared, its left edge pixel kept.
> 6. BuildLevel `$891A` (the grid, the character map, the buffers); `$9B0A` prints the score, `$9B53` the high score; WipeInPlayArea `$8CA2` brings the play area onto the screen; `$A52F` resets the overload and energy bars, sets the charging flag `$A509` and the message CHARGING LASER (DEMO in the demo).
> 7. Counts the level's bytes: D = the cells (`$40`), E = the mirrors (`$01-$10`; turning mirrors, bit 7 set, are not counted). MirrorCount `$837A` = E (the charging's scramble `$A598` turns fewer mirrors when there are 30 or more); CellsLeft `$8380` = D; the points of a burnt cell (the operand of `LD B,n` at CellPoints `$8F62`) = 12 (120 points), or 3 (30 points) when the level has 30 or more cells (levels 4, 9, 14 ... 59: 110-120 cells; 38, 58, 60).
> 8. FindLaser `$8AC1`, then MainLoop.
> Run at every level start of every play recording (165 in all).
> Witness: L1 200 (c1, c1g, cheat, controls, energy, energy-out, hof, kempston, menu, mine, nameedit, overload, practice, L x20, rzx x59).
