$9E43 RankScoreUp ; greater: one place up
$9E5A c ReadLetterKey ; Which letter key is newly pressed: carry and A = 1-26, or no carry
> Tests the 26 key codes of `LetterKeys` `$9EBD` with `TestKey` `$9EA2`. A held key: carry only when it differs from LastKey `$9B81`; KeyHeld `$9B80` = 1 while any letter is held, 0 when none.
> Witness: L1 4639 (hof, nameedit, rzx344752).
$9E62 ReadLetterLoop
$9E78 ReadLetterFound ; a new key: carry
$9E8F ReadLetterSame ; the same key as before: no carry
$9E99 ReadLetterNone
$9EA2 c TestKey ; Test one key: A = 8 x (4 - bit) + half-row, NC when it is held
> The half-row 0-7 is `$7FFE`, `$BFFE`, `$DFFE`, `$EFFE`, `$F7FE`, `$FBFE`, `$FDFE`, `$FEFE`; the high three bits count the key from the outer end of the half-row (bit 4 first). So `$21` is ENTER, `$20` SPACE, `$23` 0 (DELETE).
> Witness: L1 109958 (hof, nameedit, rzx344752).
$9EB3 TestKeyRow
$9EB8 TestKeyBit
$9EBD b LetterKeys 8 ; The key codes of A to Z for TestKey, $FF ends
> `$26` A, `$00` B, `$0F` C, `$16` D, `$15` E, `$0E` F, `$06` G, `$01` H, `$12` I, `$09` J, `$11` K, `$19` L, `$10` M, `$08` N, `$1A` O, `$22` P, `$25` Q, `$0D` R, `$1E` S, `$05` T, `$0A` U, `$07` V, `$1D` W, `$17` X, `$02` Y, `$1F` Z (the code is 8 x (4 - bit) + half-row of `TestKey`).
@res tables/letter-keys.bin

@module menu ; The title, the options panel, the demo, practice, the hall of fame's display
$9ED8 c DrawMenuScreen ; Draw the options screen into the buffer $E200 / $E500: panels, logo, options, hall of fame
> Unpacks the screen's attributes from the run-length list `$A053` (count, value; `$FF` ends) into 768 bytes at `$E200`, clears the pixel buffer `$E500`-`$FCFF` (a linear screen, 32 bytes a row), fills every character whose attribute is 2 with the dither `MenuDither` `$9F41`, then prints with `$8E3A` (records: attribute + `$80`, column, row, text; a byte with bit 7 starts the next record, `$FF` ends): "HALL OF FAME" (`$9F49`), the hall of fame's names (`$A022`) and scores (`$9FEB`), the logo and the options (`$9F59`). `$8B9B` or `$8C95` then copy the buffer to the screen.
> Called by `HallOfFame` `$9BC0` (12 runs over all recordings).
> Witness: L1 18 (c1, c1g, controls, energy-out, gameover, hof, kempston, menu, mine, nameedit, overload, rzx344752).
$9EDE MenuUnpack
$9EE6 MenuUnpackRun
$9EED MenuClearPixels ; clear the pixel buffer $E500-$FCFF
$9EFA ; 24 rows of 32 characters
$9F02 MenuDitherRow
$9F05 MenuDitherChar ; attribute 2: fill the character with the dither
$9F14 MenuDitherRows
$9F1F MenuDitherNext
$9F28 ; the texts
$9F41 b MenuDither 8 ; The dither of the options screen's background panels: $AA, $55 alternating
> Eight rows put into every character of attribute 2 by `DrawMenuScreen` `$9ED8`.
@res tables/menu-dither.bin
$9F49 b HallOfFameTitle 8 ; The text record "HALL OF FAME" at column $12, row 6, attribute $3B, $FF ends
> The records `$8E3A` prints: the first byte, bit 7 set, is the attribute + `$80`, then the column, the row, the characters; `$FF` ends the list.
@res texts/options.bin
$9F49 ; attribute $3B, column $12, row 6
$9F4C ; "HALL OF FAME", end
$9F59 b OptionsText 8 ; The logo and the options: text records for $8E3A, $FF ends
> The logo is two rows of the font's characters `a`-`z` and the brackets (`$28`, `$29`): the font holds the DEFLEKTOR logo there. Then OPTIONS and the seven lines 1 KEYBOARD, 2 SINCLAIR, 3 KEMPSTON, 4 PROTEK-AGF, 5 DEMO, 6 PRACTICE, 7 START GAME (rows 8-11, 13-14, 16, column 1, attributes `$38`, `$39`).
@res texts/options.bin
$9F59 ; the logo's top row: attribute $3B, column 8, row 1
$9F6B ; the logo's second row: attribute $3A, column 8, row 2
$9F7D ; OPTIONS: attribute $3B, column 1, row 6
$9F87 ; 1 KEYBOARD: attribute $38, row 8
$9F94 ; 2 SINCLAIR, row 9
$9FA1 ; 3 KEMPSTON, row 10
$9FAE ; 4 PROTEK-AGF, row 11
$9FBD ; 5 DEMO: attribute $39, row 13
$9FC6 ; 6 PRACTICE, row 14
$9FD3 ; 7 START GAME: attribute $38, row 16; then the end $FF
$9FE3 b NameCursorGlyph 8 ; The name entry's blinking cursor: a small diamond
> Put by `HallOfFame` `$9BC0` (at `$9CEC`, attribute `$3A`) at the next letter's place, every other 70 polls.
@res graphics/name-cursor.bin
$9FEB b HallOfFameScores 9 ; The hall of fame's six scores: text records of 9 bytes (attribute, column $18, row, six digits), $FF ends
> Rows 9-14 (`RenumberHallOfFame` `$9E00`), the top one in attribute `$3A`, the others `$38`: 090000, 075000, 060000, 045000, 030000, 015000 at the start. `HallOfFame` `$9BC0` moves records down and writes a new score; `RankScore` `$9E1F` compares with them; `PrintHighScore` `$9B53` shows the first as HIGH. `$A021` = `$FF` ends the list; the names follow at `$A022` (8-byte records, MEGA, GREAT, GOOD, SO-SO, BAD, AWFUL).
@res texts/hall-of-fame.bin
# Fragment 03-irq: $A000-$AFFF (texts, IM 2 and the COMPUTER line, the bars, sound, the ending, the gremlins, the
# title tune). The bytes $A000-$A021 are the end of the hall-of-fame score records that start at $9FEB (the other
# fragment's range): no entry here, the first entry is $A022.

@module hiscore ; The hall of fame: the score and name records, the name entry
$A022 t HallOfFameNames ; The hall of fame's six names: text records (attribute, column, row, 5 characters), $FF
> Six records of 8 bytes for the printer `$8E3A`: an attribute byte with bit 7 set (the record mark; the printer clears bit 7, so the colour is `$39`, blue on white), the column `$12`, the row `$09-$0E`, then five characters. `$FF` ends the list. They stand beside the six score records `$9FEB` (9 bytes each: attribute, column `$18`, row, six digits) printed by `$9ED8` (at `$9F2E`) on the options panel; the default names are the six ranks MEGA, GREAT, GOOD, SO-SO, BAD, AWFUL.
> A new entry (`$9BC0`): the names below the new place move down one record (`LDDR` from `$A049` to `$A051`, 8 bytes a record), the new record's five characters are cleared to spaces (`$9C6D`) and typed in by `$9E00` (which reads from `$A024`, the row byte). Evidence: written once in `rzx-344752` (the RZX player's entry, name R); read in every recording that showed the options panel (`c1g`, `menu`, `controls`, `kempston`, `mine`, `overload`, `gameover`).
@res texts/hall-of-fame.bin
