@module elements
$AAE3 b GremlinFrames 2 ; The gremlin: four frames of 16 x 16 pixels (2 bytes a row), a dithered blob that wobbles
> DrawGremlin ORs frame GremlinPhase / 32 (`$AAE3` + GremlinPhase) into the pixel buffer. Evidence: read in 54 RZX segments.
@res graphics/gremlin.bin
$AAE3 ; frame 0
$AB03 GremlinFrame1 ; frame 1
$AB23 GremlinFrame2 ; frame 2
$AB43 GremlinFrame3 ; frame 3
$AB63 b Gremlins 6 ; Eight gremlin records of 6 bytes: delay, turns, X, Y, the cell's address
> +0 delay: the passes (counted every other pass) until it appears; 0 when it walks; `$FF` would park it (no writer found). Set by the level set-up `$84B3` to `$41`, `$4B`, `$55` ... (`$41` + 10 x n) with +1 = 0, and by the cursor's kill (`$9AD2`) to `$C8`.
> +1 turns: while not 0 the gremlin stands on a mirror and turns it a step a pass (GremlinTurnMirror).
> +2, +3 X, Y: the place in characters of the play area, 0-28 and 0-16 (a cell is 2 x 2; even values are on a cell).
> +4, +5 the grid address (`$FDxx`) of the cell it stands on, +5 = 0 between cells. Written only by GremlinMove (`$AC88`, `$ACAB`) and the kill (`$9AD9`, +5 = 0): neither the set-up nor GremlinAppear touches them, so until a gremlin first steps they keep a cell from an earlier life or level. The cursor's fire (`$9AC5`) compares them with the cursor's cell, whatever the delay.
> Only GremlinCount (0-8) of them are used. Evidence: written in every level start; read in 54 RZX segments.
$AB63 ; gremlin 0
$AB69 ; gremlin 1
$AB6F ; gremlin 2
$AB75 ; gremlin 3
$AB7B ; gremlin 4
$AB81 ; gremlin 5
$AB87 ; gremlin 6
$AB8D ; gremlin 7
$AB93 b GremlinNewX ; GremlinMove's new X before it is accepted
$AB94 b GremlinNewY ; GremlinMove's new Y
$AB95 b GremlinCount ; The level's number of gremlins, 0-8: the second byte of its pair in $8385 ($84D7)
> Read by UpdateGremlins and the cursor's kill `$9AB6`. Values in the table: 0-5 and 8 (level 1: 0; level 3: 2).
$AB96 b GremlinPhase ; The animation frame x 32: + $20 each pass (0, $20, $40, $60); bit 5 clear: the delays count
$AB97 b GremlinSteps 2 ; The four moves (dX, dY): right, down, left, up
> GremlinMove picks one at random (`$8E15` L AND 3).
@res tables/gremlin-steps.bin
$AB9F c UpdateGremlins ; Each pass: count the waiting gremlins down, make them appear, walk or turn the others, draw them
> Called by the main loop (`$85A8`) when the laser is charged. GremlinPhase + `$20` (the next frame, `RES 7`). For each of GremlinCount records (IX = Gremlins, 6 bytes each): delay `$FF`: skip; delay not 0: on passes with GremlinPhase bit 5 clear count it down, at 0 GremlinAppear; delay 0: turns left: GremlinTurnMirror, else GremlinMove, then DrawGremlin.
> So the first gremlin appears 2 x 65 passes after the charge (`rzx-011752`, level 3: the charge ends at frame 382, the first GremlinAppear at 850), the next ones 20 passes apart.
> Evidence: 87 502 runs; GremlinAppear in 54 RZX segments.
> Witness: L1 112691 (c1, c1g, cheat, controls, corner, energy, energy-out, hof, kempston, menu, mine, nameedit, overload, practice, L x20, rzx x59).
$ABB3 GremlinLoop
$ABBE ; waiting: count down on every other pass
$ABD3 GremlinActive ; turns left: turn the mirror, else walk
$ABDE GremlinWalk
$ABE1 GremlinShow
$ABE4 GremlinNext
$ABED c GremlinAppear ; A random empty cell in columns 3-10, rows 0-7: the gremlin is there; BEWARE OF THE GREMLINS
> X = 2 x ((L AND 7) + 3), Y = 2 x (H AND 7) from `$8E15`; the cell `$FD12 + 17 x (row + 1) + column`. Not empty: delay = 1 (another try on the next counted pass). Empty: GremlinsMessage (MessageNumber 4) unless a more urgent one is shown, and DrawGremlin.
> Evidence: 4910 runs (54 RZX segments); the message 253 times.
> Witness: L1 5150 (hof, nameedit, L x18, rzx x54).
$AC02 ; the cell's address: $FD12 + 17 x (row + 1) + column
$AC05 GremlinCellRowLoop
$AC11 ; taken: try again next time
$AC17 GremlinWarn
$AC2D c GremlinTurnMirror ; One turn of the mirror under the gremlin: +1 or -1 of 16 angles at random
> Turns - 1; the cell at +4/+5 gets (code AND `$0F`) + 1 (one way) or (code - 2 AND `$0F`) + 1 (the other), by bit 0 of `$8E15`'s L. The beam and the redraw see the new angle on the next pass.
> Evidence: 11 188 runs (52 RZX segments).
> Witness: L1 12712 (hof, nameedit, L x15, rzx x52).
$AC45 GremlinTurnBack
$AC4D c GremlinMove ; A step in a random direction, inside the play area, onto a free cell or between cells
> New X = X + dX below 29, new Y = Y + dY below 17 (GremlinSteps). On a cell (both even): the cell's address to +4/+5; an empty cell, a cell `$40` or a mine `$41`: go; a mirror (`$01-$10`): go and stand, turns = `$8E33` AND 7; anything else: no step. Between cells: +5 = 0 and the four characters under the new place (CharMap `$E000` + 32 x (Y + 1) + X + 1) must each be below `$31` or `$74-$7B`, else no step.
> Evidence: 97 827 runs (54 RZX segments).
> Witness: L1 121430 (hof, nameedit, L x18, rzx x54).
$AC66 ; the new X
$AC6B ; the new Y
$AC75 ; both even: on a cell
$AC80 GremlinMoveRowLoop
$AC8E ; empty, $40, $41: go; a mirror: go and turn; else stay
$ACA0 ; a mirror: 0-7 turns
$ACAB GremlinBetweenCells ; the four characters under it: below $31 or $74-$7B
$ACCB GremlinCharRight
$ACD7 GremlinCharBelow
$ACE6 GremlinCharBelowRight
$ACF2 GremlinCommit ; the new place
$ACFF c DrawGremlin ; Mark the gremlin's 2 x 2 characters for redraw and OR its frame into the pixel buffer
> DrawMarks `$E300` + 32 x (Y + 1) + X + 1 (and the three next to it) = 2; the frame (`$AAE3` + GremlinPhase) ORed into PixelBuffer `$E900` + 256 x (Y + 1) + X + 1, 16 rows of 2 bytes. The +1 is the play area's border character.
> Evidence: 109 267 runs.
> Witness: L1 134460 (hof, nameedit, L x18, rzx x54).
$AD22 ; the frame
$AD36 DrawGremlinLoop
$AD46 s UnusedAD46 ; 51 bytes of 0 that no recording reads or writes
> No instruction in the image addresses them.

@module sound
$AD79 c TitleTune ; The title tune with the interrupt off: 176 notes from TitleTuneData, again and again until a key
> `DI`; IX = TitleTuneData, B = `$B0` notes; each note: H, L, D the three voices' periods, BC = `$1770` (6000) loops of PlayNote. After each note the keyboard (all half rows): a key ends it with `EI`. After the last note it starts again.
> Called by TitleScreen `$A754`, the pause `$97AE` (the "pause's tune"), the ending `$88AA`. One note takes 677 051-733 334 T (9.7-10.5 frames), the tune 35.4 s (SkoolKit's simulator, work/ann/03-irq/simtime.py).
> Evidence: 12 runs (`c1g`, `controls`, `gameover`, `kempston`, `menu`, `mine`, `overload`, `rzx-344752`).
> Witness: L1 17 (c1, c1g, controls, ending, energy-out, gameover, hof, kempston, menu, mine, nameedit, overload, rzx344752).
$AD7A TitleTuneRestart
$AD80 TitleTuneNoteLoop
$ADA5 TitleTuneEnd
$ADA7 c PlayNote ; The inner loop of a note: three counters; each that reaches 0 sends a short pulse and reloads
> H, L, D count down once a loop; at 0 the speaker goes off (`OUT` 0) for C = 15 `DJNZ` loops and back on (`$10`) for 16 - C = 1, then the counter is reloaded from the note (IX+0, +1, +2). So each voice is a train of short pulses at 3.5 MHz / (about 108 T x period): `$42` about 490 Hz, `$84` about 245 Hz. BC on the stack counts the note's loops (`ADE8`); `NOP NOP` pad the loop.
> Evidence: 6 919 984 runs.
> Witness: L1 10535890 (c1, c1g, controls, ending, energy, energy-out, gameover, hof, kempston, menu, mine, nameedit, overload, practice, rzx344752).
$ADB3 NoteVoice1Off
$ADBB NoteVoice1On
$ADC0 NoteVoice2
$ADC7 NoteVoice2Off
$ADCF NoteVoice2On
$ADD4 NoteVoice3
$ADDB NoteVoice3Off
$ADE3 NoteVoice3On
$ADE8 NoteLoopEnd ; the note's loop count (BC on the stack)
$ADF0 b TitleTuneData 3 ; The title tune: 176 notes of three periods (voice 1, 2, 3), up to $AFFF
> 528 bytes, `$ADF0-$AFFF`; 54 different periods from `$2B` to `$E2`. Read by TitleTune. Evidence: read in `c1g`, `controls`, `gameover`, `kempston`, `menu`, `mine`, `overload`, `rzx-344752`.
@res tables/title-tune.bin

@module levels ; The levels: the data and the builder
$B000 b LevelData 15 ; The 64 level slots: 135 cell codes each (9 rows of 15), slot = Level, at $B000 + $87 x slot
> BuildLevel `$891A` (`$891E`: `LD HL,$B000`, then `$87` added Level times) copies a slot's 9 rows of 15 into the grid `$FD23` (17 bytes a row: two guards). Slots 0-59 are levels 1-60, slots 60-62 the practice levels P1-P3, slot 63 is never set up.
> A cell code: `$00` empty; `$01-$10` a mirror at 16 angles; `$11-$18` a cyan polariser and `$19-$20` a yellow one at 8 angles; `$21-$2F` an absorbing and `$30-$3E` a reflecting wall, by the quarters it fills; `$40` a cell to burn; `$41` a mine; `$42` a prism; `$47-$4A` the laser facing east, south, west, north; `$4B-$4E` the receiver open to the east, south, west, north; `$50-$57` the fibre-optic ends, pairs `$50/$51`, `$52/$53`, `$54/$55`, `$56/$57`. Bit 7 set on a mirror or a polariser (`$81-$A0`): it turns by itself, one step a pass (RenderGrid `$8960`). Each level holds one laser and one receiver.
> Slot 63 is level 23 with five mirrors at other angles (rows 0, 4, 8): a draft or a copy left over. Slot 60 (P1) is read by `practice` (`work/docs/dfA/rec/practice`); slots 61-63 by no recording. Editable form: levels/levels.txt.
